  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings rely on self-reported survey data from a single megacity under unique local car-ownership restrictions.",
      "what": "Using a survey of 5,967 households across Shanghai resettlement communities, this study leveraged a quasi-random housing allocation scheme alongside double machine learning and causal mediation analysis to evaluate travel behavior changes. Deterioration in the built environment—specifically reduced land-use mix, lower accessibility, and decreased transit service provision—decreased active travel while increasing public transit reliance. The effect operates through constrained adjustments in car and non-motorized vehicle ownership under institutional motorization limits.",
      "why": "The findings counter standard Western suburbanization models by demonstrating that degraded peripheral environments can force higher transit dependency rather than car use when institutional barriers limit automobile acquisition."
